    "q-93047": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93047",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the domain of $n(x)=-2\\sqrt[4]{x-2}-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $n(x)=-2\\sqrt[4]{x-2}-1,$ we replace $x$ with $x-2$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\nx-2\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=n(x)$ is $x\\in [2,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=n(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14239886/assets/b9d1df320fa9d2664365c6990df105456cfd6614209e1421224b1202b777f0cb.png)",
