    "q-115816": {
      "dom_id": "step-q115816",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the domain of $t(x)=-5\\sqrt[6]{6x+3}-8.$",
      "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\sqrt[6]{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $t(x)=-5\\sqrt[6]{6x+3}-8,$ we replace $x$ with $6x+3$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n6x+3\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 6x & \\ge -3 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=t(x)$ is $x\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )\\,.$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=t(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14239886/assets/7407b43998fe6c0f0d4e3151d0c46b4495796bc147754bdd0187517e2f783e86.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
